  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 247 PM CDT Fri Jul 31 2026\n\nA nearly stacked low pressure system will continue to drift \nsoutheastward into central Iowa through the remainder of the \nafternoon, and with the surface low deepening, look for winds to \nincrease a bit more mainly east of I-29 through the early evening \nhours. Most spots top out with wind gusts between 35 and 40 \nmph, but isolated pockets of gusts to around 45 mph will be \npossible over northwest Iowa through about 7 to 8 pm this \nevening. This should remain in short duration and isolated and \nso decided against wind headlines. Moisture also continues to \nwrap around this low which will keep mainly light rain in the \nforecast through this evening primarily east of I-29. Soundings \nshow a little bit of elevated instability over parts of \nnorthwest Iowa, so can't rule out a rumble of thunder this \nafternoon, but chances for this are looking less and less \nlikely. The last of the showers should exit northwest Iowa by 10\npm to midnight tonight, leading to dry conditions heading into \nthe weekend.\n\nPatchy fog is possible where winds can go very light to calm by \nearly tomorrow morning, but it does look like winds should remain \nelevated enough to prevent widespread development. Trends will be \nmonitored. Otherwise, a pleasant day is in store for tomorrow \nwith highs in the upper 70s to lower 80s with dew points down \ninto the mid 50s west to low 60s east. These lower dew points \nwill lead to more comfortable conditions out there especially \nwhen compared to the past few days. The humidity relief will be \nshort-lived however, as southerly flow returns on Sunday and \nallows dew points to rise through the 60s and potentially as \nhigh as the lower 70s in the afternoon. It won't be nearly as \nhot as it has been though as highs will still remain mainly in \nthe 80s with a few pockets of highs near 90 in south-central \nSouth Dakota. Those hotter areas will likely be offset from the \nhigher dew points though, so heat indices won't get too high. \nTemperatures will continue to warm on Monday and this will be \nout ahead of our next cold frontal passage. This cold front may \nbring a chance of stronger thunderstorms to the area, but the \novernight timing of the frontal passage and the fact the better \nupper level support will be displaced to our north leads to a \nlow confidence forecast for severe weather.\n\nThis frontal boundary may stall out or slow down near or south \nof the Highway 20 corridor as a large upper wave pivots slowly \nacross the region. This will keep rain and storm chances in the\nforecast mainly south of I-90 through the day on Tuesday and \npotentially into Wednesday as well depending on where the \nfrontal boundary stalls. Will have to watch this for perhaps \nsome heavy rain potential as some of the lower resolution global\nmodels such as the 31.12Z ECMWF and Canadian model 48 hour QPF \nalready shows a stripe of 1 to 3+ inches either near Highway 20 \nor near I-80 depending on the model. So we'll see what the \nhigher resolution models come up with as we get closer, but \nthese areas south of I-90 didn't receive as much rain with this\nmost recent system, so the flood threat may still remain low\nanyway. Cooler weather immediately follows the cold frontal \npassage, with highs likely only staying in the 70s Tuesday and \nWednesday before gradually warming up through the end of next \nweek.",
